-- The credential a merchant's till holds.
--
-- Generated by `pnpm --filter @vyloop/db db:generate` and reviewed by hand, like
-- `0011_material_odin.sql`. It is the only SQL this feature ships.
--
-- ## The hole this table closes
--
-- `workers/growth/src/helpers.ts:244` (`requireCounterSubject`) gates
-- `POST /v1/loyalty/checkins` and `POST /v1/loyalty/passes/resolve` on a JWT
-- subject of the form `pos:<terminalId>` with a non-empty remainder. That guard
-- exists because those routes had no guard at all, and the absence was the
-- defect: `POST /v1/auth/customer/token` is public and mints `role: 'customer'`,
-- so any customer could report a fabricated sale and have it credited at whatever
-- the tenant's earn rule made of an `amount_cents` they chose.
--
-- The guard was written correctly and there was **no way for anything to satisfy
-- it**. No issuer in the system minted a `pos:` subject, so both counter routes
-- had exactly two reachable states: a counter, of which there were none, and a
-- refusal. The routes were finished and the door had no key.
--
-- This table is that key. `POST /v1/auth/pos/token` exchanges a terminal
-- credential for a JWT whose subject is `pos:<id>`.
--
-- ## Why the secret is stored hashed and only hashed
--
-- Because the merchant is shown it once, at creation, and it is never readable
-- again. This database therefore has to be able to answer "does this secret
-- match" without being able to answer "what is the secret" -- which is exactly
-- what `users.password_hash` already does, and the exchange verifies with the
-- same `verifyPassword` from `@vyloop/db` rather than a second implementation.
-- `password.ts` documents why that module exists: three copies of one PBKDF2 is
-- three places for an iteration count to drift, and a terminal credential is a
-- credential like any other.
--
-- The cost, stated rather than hidden: an exchange pays a 100k-iteration
-- derivation, which is more work than a high-entropy random secret needs. It is
-- paid anyway, because the alternative is a second hash format living beside the
-- first and every later argument about iteration counts becoming an argument about
-- two schemes.
--
-- The rejected alternative was a fast digest (SHA-256 of a 32-byte random secret),
-- which is the textbook answer for machine credentials and is measurably cheaper
-- per exchange. It loses because this system now has two hash formats, two
-- upgrade paths, and no single place that answers "how strong is a credential in
-- this database". One scheme, for a table that holds one credential per till.
--
-- ## Why `revoked_at` is a timestamp and not a delete
--
-- Because "this terminal reported a sale at 14:02" has to stay answerable after
-- somebody revokes the key. Deleting the row leaves a `checkins.payment_id` that
-- traces back to no credential, and the checkin's value as an audit record is
-- precisely that trace. Revocation changes what the credential can *do*, never
-- whether it happened.
--
-- `NULL` means live. Rejected alternatives:
--
--   A. A `revoked` boolean beside `revoked_at`. Two columns that can disagree, so
--      every query has to decide which one it believes.
--   B. Delete the row. Destroys the audit link, above.
--   C. A sentinel timestamp (`'epoch'`) instead of NULL. Makes the ordinary state
--      a value that has to be excluded from every query meaning "currently
--      valid", which is how a revoked terminal keeps working.
--
-- ## Why `last_seen_at` exists and is nullable
--
-- `NULL` until the terminal has ever exchanged a credential, which is the honest
-- answer for a terminal that has been installed and never used. It is a
-- diagnostic -- "is this till alive" -- and nothing reads it to make an
-- authorization decision, because a freshness signal that gates a login is a
-- clock problem turned into an outage.
--
-- ## Why `business_id` is the tenant, cascading from `tenants.id`
--
-- The same rule as `checkins.business_id`, for the same reason: it comes from the
-- credential, never from the request body. A credential minted for one merchant
-- must not be able to report a sale against another, and the scope of the token
-- is what prevents that. Cascade, like every other `business_id` in this schema:
-- a credential for a tenant that no longer exists is not a credential, it is a row
-- that would still verify a secret for whoever kept it.
--
-- ## Why there is no unique constraint on `(business_id, name)`
--
-- Considered and rejected. Two terminals genuinely can share a name -- a merchant
-- re-numbering their tills, a second counter at one till for a second payment
-- method -- and refusing the second row would be a product decision about
-- somebody's counter layout made by a database constraint. A duplicate name is
-- confusing; a refused installation is a sale that cannot be reported, and this
-- table exists so a sale always can be. `idx_pos_terminals_business` orders the
-- listing so the order is stable, and claims nothing about uniqueness.
--
-- ## Why there is no index on this table
--
-- The only query against it today is the exchange, which looks a terminal up by
-- primary key, which the primary key already indexes. The one query that would
-- want an index is "which terminals does this merchant have", and no code runs
-- it -- registration and listing are out of scope, so it has no reader. An index
-- is paid for on every insert in order to speed up a query nobody issues, and a
-- table with one row per till is a table a sequential scan answers perfectly.
--
-- That is a statement about today and not a rule. When the listing route lands,
-- `idx_pos_terminals_business` goes in the table's extra-config callback in
-- `schema.ts` **and** the table joins `INDEXED_TABLES` in `indexes.ts` in the
-- same change: `migration-sql.test.ts` asserts that the `CREATE INDEX` statements
-- in this history are exactly what that registry reports, so an index declared in
-- one file and not the other fails the build rather than drifting quietly.
--
-- ## What is NOT here
--
-- **Registration of a terminal is not implemented.** Nothing in this repository
-- creates a row in this table: no route, no script, no dashboard screen. So no
-- terminal can be created yet, and `POST /v1/auth/pos/token` is unreachable in
-- practice until one can. That is deliberate scope, and the verification path was
-- built complete anyway -- an unknown id, a wrong secret and a revoked terminal
-- are all refused -- so the moment a writer exists the exchange works without
-- being touched.
--
-- When that writer lands, the secret is generated, hashed with `hashPassword`,
-- written here and returned **once** in the creation response. It cannot be
-- recovered afterwards, by design and by the absence of any read path for it.
--
-- ## Not applied here
--
-- `drizzle-kit migrate` against production is forbidden in this repository: the
-- ledger has drift and running the migrator would apply migrations twice. A
-- person applies this by hand, after review. This file is left on disk, unrun.
CREATE TABLE "pos_terminals" (
	"id" uuid PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid() NOT NULL,
	"business_id" uuid NOT NULL,
	"name" text NOT NULL,
	"key_hash" text NOT NULL,
	"last_seen_at" timestamp with time zone,
	"revoked_at" timestamp with time zone,
	"created_at" timestamp with time zone DEFAULT now() NOT NULL
);
--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "pos_terminals" ADD CONSTRAINT "pos_terminals_business_id_tenants_id_fk" FOREIGN KEY ("business_id") REFERENCES "public"."tenants"("id") ON DELETE cascade ON UPDATE no action;